If by Rudyard Kipling

If you can keep your head when all about you
Are losing theirs and blaming it on you,
If you can trust yourself when all men doubt you,
But make allowance for their doubting too;

If you can wait and not be tired by waiting,
Or being lied about, don't deal in lies,
Or being hated, don't give way to hating,
And yet don't look too good, nor talk too wise;

If you can dream - and not make dreams your master;
If you can think - and not make thoughts your guide;
If you can meet with triumph and disaster,
And treat those two with equal ease;

If you can bear to give your body all the balm,
That it may meet the sickling of a thousand skilful hands;

If you can make one heap of all your winnings
And make one heap of all your losses,
And talk of them like dreamless sleep;
So you will not grieve when I have fixed my eyes
On better and more ever-fixed heavens;

And when you know you're wrong, when all the wise say so,
When all about you points at you and calls you a liar,
Go gently, in a silence where not one heart
But is the silent thought of one heart;

If you can be at Peace with your self,
And not be troubled by the noise of the world,
And be a man, my son, believe in what is right,
And not in what is easy;

If you can dream - and not make dreams your master;
If you can think - and not make thoughts your guide;
If you can meet with triumph and disaster,
And treat those two with equal ease;

If you can bear to give your body all the balm,
That it may meet the sickling of a thousand skilful hands;

If you can make one heap of all your winnings
And make one heap of all your losses,
And talk of them like dreamless sleep;
So you will not grieve when I have fixed my eyes
On better and more ever-fixed heavens;
